  "psychology": {
    "note": "How a typeface class tends to be read, as weights against the product traits (same vocabulary as traits.json). Research on type personality is thin and most of it used a handful of fonts, so these decide where to start, never what the user must choose.",
    "sources": {
      "S2006": "Shaikh, Chaparro & Fox (2006). Perception of fonts: perceived personality traits and uses. Usability News 8(1). 561 participants, 20 fonts: serif fonts scored highest on stable, practical, mature, formal; script/fun fonts on youthful, happy, creative, casual, cuddly; display fonts (Impact, Rockwell Extra Bold) on masculine, assertive, coarse; monospaced fonts on dull, plain, unimaginative, conforming; sans serif fonts on nothing in particular.",
      "H2004": "Henderson, Giese & Cote (2004). Impression management using typeface design. Journal of Marketing 68(4), 60-72. Design dimensions of a typeface (elaborate, harmony, natural, flourish, weight, compressed) drive whether it is read as pleasing, engaging, reassuring or prominent.",
      "G2013": "Grohmann, Giese & Parkman (2013). Using type font characteristics to communicate brand personality of new brands. Journal of Brand Management 20, 389-403. The font a brand name is set in shifts the brand's perceived personality, independently of colour.",
      "convention": "Design convention without a study behind it in this file. Weighted lower."
    },
    "classes": {
      "neutral-sans": {
        "means": "plain modern sans: says little, gets out of the way",
        "traits": {
          "competence": 0.8,
          "trust": 0.8,
          "technical": 0.5,
          "calm": 0.5
        },
        "source": "S2006"
      },
      "geometric-sans": {
        "means": "circles and straight lines: clean, contemporary",
        "traits": {
          "competence": 0.6,
          "calm": 0.6,
          "trust": 0.5,
          "creativity": 0.3,
          "sophistication": 0.3
        },
